<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!--
  Layout of the OntoBoard Sheets tab.

  One view, the whole window. That is the point of the tab: the sheet editor spent five releases
  under the canvas at 45% of a 72%-wide column, which on the twenty-six-sheet knowledge graph it
  was built against showed three or four of twenty-six columns and wrapped its own toolbar.

  THE ROOT MUST BE A SPLITTER, even for one view. A bare <CNode> here parses - the SAX handler
  ignores what it does not recognise - and then throws while the tab is being built. Protege
  catches that inside TabbedWorkspace.addTab, REMOVES the tab from the tabbed pane, puts an
  error panel in its place and logs nothing; addTabForPlugin still hands back the tab, so the
  next call fails with "component not found in tabbed pane" and nothing says why. The host
  smoke caught it; 2,276 unit tests did not. Protege's own one-view tab -
  viewconfig-explanationtab.xml in protege-editor-owl - is a VSNode with a single splits value
  and one CNode inside, which is exactly the shape below.

  Deliberately no entity column beside it. A spreadsheet is wide - the identifier column alone
  is 320px because the MatWerk identifiers are 46 characters - and the views that would go there
  are one click away in the OntoBoard tab and in Protege's own Entities tab. Space is the whole
  reason this tab exists; spending a fifth of it on a tree would be giving back what it was made
  for.
-->
<layout>
    <VSNode splits="1.0">
        <CNode>
            <Component label="Sheets">
                <Property id="pluginId" value="${project.artifactId}.SheetEditorView"/>
            </Component>
        </CNode>
    </VSNode>
</layout>
